A||||4||Alex2004||Sqeaking right front caliper (not worn pads)||||4||
Z||000000||Alex2004||12-16-2001||07:47 PM||||i noticed this a few days ago because the pedal felt wierd also.  When stopping fairly hard the pedal felt umm...it's hard to describe, not pulsating or spongy but it feels...don't know how to describe it, anyways, my front right caliper when pushing the brakes about half way down makes a squeaking noise/doesn't matter if car is completely stopped in park, or rolling to a stop sign, whenever pushing on it hard i can hear it making a squeaking noise. and that's whenever the pedal does what it does.  It's like it kinda moves w/ the squeak.  It's hard to describe.  LoL anyways, do any of you know whats cause this. the caliper retaining bolts are greased w/ brake grease (did that today) pads are good.  I  have an 84 se.  thanks for any help!<P>Alex...||||reg||4
Z||000001||GTDude||12-16-2001||08:12 PM||||Sounds like the caliper is stuck and the caliper is flexing on the rotor making the squealing noise.  I'll bet the pedal feels kinda hard...not quite spongy but definitely weird.  It prob doesn't pull one way or the other cause the caliper is frozen so close to the rotor and it flexes enough to apply the pads.  Hope this helps.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex2004||12-16-2001||08:36 PM||||thanks for the info.  the pedal really doesn't feel hard but whenever your stopping it just feels wierd because it kidna moves alittle like a slight jump. but barly noticeable.  it doesn't squeal thought just a squeak like say door hinges in need of wd 40 or something.  and there was room between the disc and the pads.  I'll look into replacing it though. i plan on replacing all 4 eventually! thanks again!<P>alex..||||reg||1
Z||000003||GTDude||12-16-2001||09:00 PM||||No problem alex......anytime!<P>Phil Pettus||||reg||10
Z||000004||Blacktree||12-17-2001||02:13 AM||||About 6 months ago, the left front caliper on my 87 Fiero started doing just what you described. Push the brake pedal until the caliper had the pads firmly planted on the disc, then push the pedal a little more and there was this little squeaking sound. And I could "feel" the squeaking through the brake pedal.<P>A couple months later the left front caliper decided to stick in the "brakes on" position, caused the brake fluid to boil, and almost put me into the back end of a box truck.<P>So the moral of the story is that it's probably a good time to rebuild or replace that caliper before you experience some "adventures in brakeless driving".   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A><p>[This message has been edited by Blacktree (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||1
